While both compilers aim for IEEE 754 compliance, they diverge significantly when you introduce `-ffast-math`. This flag allows the compiler to ignore strict IEEE rules, enabling optimizations like reassociation, ignoring NaNs/Infs, and—critically for your case—using **vendor-specific math library shortcuts (libm vs. sleef/svml) and different instruction selection (FMA vs. separate multiply-add).**

Because `sin()` and `exp()` are transcendental functions, they are implemented as polynomial approximations. GCC and Clang may link against different versions of `glibc`'s `libm` or use different internal intrinsic expansions (especially when targeting AVX-512), leading to "drift" in the last few bits of the mantissa.

### The Simulation Test
To demonstrate this, I have designed a "drift" test: an iterative loop where the result of one calculation is fed back into the next. This amplifies tiny precision differences into visible divergence.

**Test Code (`precision_test.cpp`):**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>

int main() {
    double x = 0.123456789;
    // Iterate to amplify the floating point drift
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) {
        x = std::sin(x) + std::exp(-x) * 0.5;
        // Keep x bounded to prevent overflow
        if (x > 1.0) x -= 1.0;
        if (x < -1.0) x += 1.0;
    }
    std::cout << std::setprecision(17) << x << std::endl;
    return 0;
